    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Patients with acute hypercalcemia usually have either acute hyperparathyroidism or metastatic breast carcinoma with multiple bony metastases. These patients develop severe headaches, bone pain, thirst, emesis, and polyuria. Unless treatment is instituted promptly, the symptoms may be rapidly fatal. Immediate correction of the associated deficit of extracellular fluid volume is the most important step in treatment. When effective, this results in the lowering of the serum calcium level by dilution. Once extracellular fluid volume has been replaced, furosemide is effective treatment. Hemodialysis may also be employed, but its effect is less rapid. Mithramycin is very useful in controlling metastatic bone disease, but its effect is slow, and it cannot be depended upon when the patient has acute hypercalcemia.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Mithramycin is not the first-line treatment for acute hypercalcemia. While it can be useful in controlling metastatic bone disease, its effect is slow, and it cannot be relied upon for the prompt management of acute hypercalcemia. Therefore, it is not the recommended initial step.\nMithramycin, also known as mitomycin, is an antineoplastic antibiotic with several mechanisms of action. Its primary mode of action involves binding to DNA and inhibiting the synthesis of RNA and, to a lesser extent, DNA.\nMechanisms of Action:\nDNA Binding: Mithramycin binds to the DNA molecule by intercalating between the base pairs. This binding interferes with the normal functioning of DNA, disrupting the structure and stability of the DNA double helix.\nInhibition of RNA Synthesis: By binding to DNA, mithramycin inhibits the synthesis of RNA. RNA is essential for protein synthesis, and interference with its production disrupts the cellular machinery involved in protein formation.\nInhibition of DNA Synthesis: While mithramycin primarily inhibits RNA synthesis, it also has some inhibitory effect on DNA synthesis. This contributes to its overall antineoplastic activity by impeding the replication of cancer cells.\nAntiangiogenic Effects: Mithramycin has been reported to exhibit antiangiogenic properties, meaning it can inhibit the formation of new blood vessels. This is relevant in cancer treatment as tumors require a blood supply for growth and metastasis.",
      "B": "Patients with acute hypercalcemia usually have either acute hyperparathyroidism or metastatic breast carcinoma with multiple bony metastases. These patients develop severe headaches, bone pain, thirst, emesis, and polyuria. Unless treatment is instituted promptly, the symptoms may be rapidly fatal. Immediate correction of the associated deficit of extracellular fluid volume is the most important step in treatment. When effective, this results in the lowering of the serum calcium level by dilution. Once extracellular fluid volume has been replaced, furosemide is effective treatment. Hemodialysis may also be employed, but its effect is less rapid. Mithramycin is very useful in controlling metastatic bone disease, but its effect is slow, and it cannot be depended upon when the patient has acute hypercalcemia.",
      "C": "Furosemide is effective in treating hypercalcemia after correction of extracellular fluid volume. Once intravascular volume is adequately replaced, furosemide can enhance renal calcium excretion. However, it is not the first step and is typically used as an adjunctive therapy.",
      "D": "Hemodialysis may be considered in severe cases of hypercalcemia, but it is not the initial step. It is generally reserved for situations where other measures have failed or in the presence of life-threatening hypercalcemia that requires rapid intervention. It is not the first-line choice."
    },
