    "stem": "Initial management of a pyogenic liver does NOT include .",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Surgical drainage and/or resection"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Percutaneous fine-needle aspiration and culture"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Treatment of the underlying cause"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "The current cornerstones of treatment include correction of the underlying cause and IV antibiotic therapy. Empiric antibiotic therapy should cover gram-negative and anaerobic organisms; percutaneous needle aspiration and culture of the aspirate may be useful in guiding subsequent antibiotic therapy. IV antibiotic therapy should be continued for at least 8 weeks and can be expected to be effective in 80% to 90% of patients. Placement of a percutaneous drainage catheter is beneficial only for a minority of patients, as most pyogenic abscesses are quite viscous and catheter drainage is often ineffective. Surgical drainage either via the laparoscopic or open approach may become necessary if initial therapies fail.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The current cornerstones of treatment include correction of the underlying cause and IV antibiotic therapy. Empiric antibiotic therapy should cover gram-negative and anaerobic organisms; percutaneous needle aspiration and culture of the aspirate may be useful in guiding subsequent antibiotic therapy. IV antibiotic therapy should be continued for at least 8 weeks and can be expected to be effective in 80% to 90% of patients. Placement of a percutaneous drainage catheter is beneficial only for a minority of patients, as most pyogenic abscesses are quite viscous and catheter drainage is often ineffective. Surgical drainage either via the laparoscopic or open approach may become necessary if initial therapies fail.",
      "B": "Percutaneous fine-needle aspiration and culture is a part of the initial management. This procedure is useful in guiding subsequent antibiotic therapy. Aspiration of the abscess content allows for identification of the causative organisms, helping tailor antibiotic treatment to the specific pathogen.",
      "C": "The correction of the underlying cause is highlighted as one of the cornerstones of initial management. Identifying and addressing the cause contributing to the development of the pyogenic liver abscess is crucial for a comprehensive and effective approach.",
      "D": "Broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics are a crucial component of the initial management of pyogenic liver abscess. The empiric antibiotic therapy should cover gram-negative and anaerobic organisms. IV antibiotic therapy is recommended for at least 8 weeks and has a high efficacy rate (80% to 90%) in treating the condition."
    },
