    "stem": "Which surgeon was awarded the Nobel prize in Physiology or Medicine for his work on the “physiology, pathology, and surgery of the thyroid gland”?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Harvey Cushing"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "John Hunter"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Emil Kocher"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Theodore Billroth"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine was awarded to Emil Kocher in 1909. In addition to his research on the physiology of the thyroid, Kocher's operative methods greatly reduced the mortality risk of thyroidectomy. The Kocher clamp was designed to prevent hemorrhage from the hypervascular gland during thyroidectomy.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Harvey Cushing was a prominent American neurosurgeon who made significant contributions to the field of neurosurgery. He is best known for developing and advancing techniques in brain surgery, particularly the treatment of brain tumors. He was not awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for thyroid-related work.",
      "B": "John Hunter was an 18th-century Scottish surgeon and anatomist. He made important contributions to the understanding of anatomy and surgery. However, he did not receive the Nobel Prize for work on the thyroid gland.",
      "C": "The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine was awarded to Emil Kocher in 1909. In addition to his research on the physiology of the thyroid, Kocher's operative methods greatly reduced the mortality risk of thyroidectomy. The Kocher clamp was designed to prevent hemorrhage from the hypervascular gland during thyroidectomy.",
      "D": "Theodore Billroth, an Austrian surgeon, was a pioneering figure in the field of surgery in the 19th century. While he made significant contributions to general surgery, he did not receive the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for thyroid-related work."
    },
