    "id": "superexam-surgery-0610",
    "subject": "surgery",
    "originalNumber": 610,
    "stem": "After an uncomplicated abdominal surgery\n(appendectomy), the 35-year old patient is still on\nNPO during his 5th hospital stay due to inability to\ntolerate feeding. After how many hours/days\nshould normal intestinal motility be observed in\nits earliest post-operatively?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "12 hours"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "24 hours"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "48 hours"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "72 hours"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "4 days"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Following most abdominal operations or\ninjuries, the motility of the gastrointestinal tract\nis transiently impaired. Among the proposed\nmechanisms responsible for this dysmotility are\nsurgical stress-induced sympathetic reflexes,\ninflammatory response-mediator release, and\nanesthetic/analgesic effects; each of which can\ninhibit intestinal motility. The return of normal\nmotility generally follows a characteristic\ntemporal sequence, with small-intestinal\nmotility returning to normal within the first 24\nhours after laparotomy and gastric and colonic\nmotility returning to normal by 48 hours and 3\nto 5 days, respectively. Resolution of ileus may\nbe delayed in the presence of other factors\ncapable of inciting ileus such as the presence of\nintra-abdominal abscesses or electrolyte\nabnormalities.",
